WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.540
Nothing in life can prepare you for the raw heat&nbsp;
of a house on fire. Throwing an arm up in front of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:11.880
his eyes and clasping a handkerchief to his mouth,&nbsp;
Robert Chetford hacked up half of the contents of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.880 --> 00:00:17.460
his lungs. Glancing at the material, he saw that&nbsp;
his phlegm was black, poisoned by the smoke.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:22.380
A beam collapsed in front of him, sending&nbsp;
a fresh wave of burning air into his face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:27.240
so hot that it felt like it was going&nbsp;
to scorch his ears off. He had to run,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.800
he had to get out of the house, but he&nbsp;
knew he couldn't. His mother was upstairs.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:37.140
Robert barreled his way through the flames,&nbsp;
shouldering open doors and stumbling along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.580
hallways. The air was so thick with&nbsp;
smoke that he could hardly breathe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:46.200
so he collapsed to the floor and started&nbsp;
to crawl on his stomach. He reached the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:51.060
first step of the grand staircase and&nbsp;
started to heave himself up one at a time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.060 --> 00:00:56.400
feeling the heat of the house threatening to&nbsp;
engulf him more with every inch he climbed.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.620
But he hadn't made it more than five steps&nbsp;
before there was an enormous creaking sound,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:06.900
and the whole staircase collapsed beneath&nbsp;
him. Sparks rushed up into the air and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.000
swirled around the room. Flames licked at the&nbsp;
walls all around him. As Robert looked around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.480
at the collapsed staircase, he knew that it was&nbsp;
hopeless. With tears streaming down his cheeks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.480 --> 00:01:21.420
he pushed himself forward&nbsp;
and ran out of the house.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:25.620
The firemen swarmed past him, filling&nbsp;
the building and shouting to one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:30.780
but Robert just stood there, staring up at&nbsp;
the top-floor window. On the other side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.200
the glass, surrounded by billowing&nbsp;
smoke, he looked up at his mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:41.100
sitting as she always did on her rocking chair,&nbsp;
utterly motionless as the house burned around her…

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.340
In an undisclosed facility in&nbsp;
the United States of America,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.540
you will find what may well be the&nbsp;
lowest-security containment cell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:54.600
in the entire SCP Foundation. There is no&nbsp;
heavily armored door, no bulletproof glass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:02:00.120
no machine guns about to drop down from the&nbsp;
ceiling at any point. In fact, there's very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.920
little in the way of containment at all, they&nbsp;
often leave the door to the cell wide open.

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.500
There is a window, but on the other side of it&nbsp;
isn't a crowd of researchers, security guards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.400
and agents ready to spring into&nbsp;
action at a moment's notice. It's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:21.180
just a normal double-glazed window that looks&nbsp;
out across a vista of fields, trees, and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:30.120
couple of power lines. Facing the window is an old&nbsp;
armchair in which the Concrete Man sits. SCP-014.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:35.520
He spends all of his day, every day,&nbsp;
staring out of the window. This view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:39.900
specifically was chosen because very&nbsp;
little changes in it. Night turns to day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.540
the trees change with the seasons, and it&nbsp;
rains occasionally, but there are no roads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:51.840
no new buildings springing up, nothing to&nbsp;
distinguish it from how it looked in 1937.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:57.000
Beside him is a small table, home to a&nbsp;
record player. In the corner of the room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:02.940
there's an antique set of shelves that house&nbsp;
three dozen records, all dated from the 1930s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:09.000
or earlier. All day, every day, the music&nbsp;
plays out of the old speaker. Every so often,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:13.860
as researchers walk up and down the corridor,&nbsp;
if they hear that there isn't any music playing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:19.200
they'll come into the room and change the record&nbsp;
over for a new one. The newer researchers check&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:26.280
in with SCP-014 to see if he's okay. He rarely&nbsp;
responds, and so before long, they stop asking.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.080
You would think, therefore, that the&nbsp;
concrete man is elderly, approaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.060
the twilight years of his life. But the man&nbsp;
who sits there in his chair, staring out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:42.600
the window and listening to music from almost&nbsp;
100 years ago, appears to be just 30 years old.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:42.990
*

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:46.980
Friends of Robert Chetford reported&nbsp;
a noticeable change in his demeanor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.940
following the fire and the death of his&nbsp;
mother. He had lost his father not long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.680
before to a workplace accident. William&nbsp;
Chetford had been a construction worker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.440
climbing up the girders of New York's emerging&nbsp;
skyline, pouring concrete and piling bricks.

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:05.940
He had been working on a new building, one&nbsp;
that was set to be the tallest in the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:10.920
called the Empire State Building. Day&nbsp;
after day, he would climb the cranes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.760
teeter precariously over the&nbsp;
dizzying drops. Any slight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.920
mistake and he would plummet hundreds of&nbsp;
feet, often to the busy streets below.

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:24.900
Sure enough, one day, William Chetford's&nbsp;
worst fears came to life. He was walking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:30.120
on a gangplank that wobbled and bent with every&nbsp;
step he took. On his shoulders was a metal bar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:35.340
with a bucket of concrete dangling precariously&nbsp;
from each tip. He walked this route every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:39.540
but clearly, that day proved&nbsp;
too much for the wood to take.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:44.160
The board splintered and split beneath&nbsp;
him, plunging him down and down through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.840
the scaffolding. William desperately reached&nbsp;
out an arm and managed to grasp the edge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.300
a girder. He hung there helplessly, staring up&nbsp;
at his colleagues high above him. He cried out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:58.800
for help, and upon seeing him, they began&nbsp;
to scramble down level by level to try and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:04.080
reach him. He just needed to hang on for a&nbsp;
moment longer, and they would rescue him.

00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:09.180
But one of them knocked over a bucket of concrete.&nbsp;
William Chetford could do nothing but watch as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:14.160
the heavy sludge slowly tumbled towards his&nbsp;
face. He tried to dodge it, but it was no use.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:18.600
The company told Mrs. Chetford that&nbsp;
this had been William's saving grace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:23.640
that he had been unconscious for the&nbsp;
long fall down to the streets below. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.640 --> 00:05:28.200
fellow workers knew that wasn't the case.&nbsp;
They heard his screams all the way down.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:34.320
Mrs. Chetford didn't believe any of it. They were&nbsp;
lying, all of them—the company, his co-workers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:40.140
everybody. She knew what had really killed him.&nbsp;
It was the same thing that was killing her slowly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:44.760
the same thing that had been passed&nbsp;
onto their son: concrete poisoning.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:50.100
She'd approached the Wall Street Journal, New York&nbsp;
Times, and any outlets she could, but none of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:56.040
were remotely interested in the story. There were&nbsp;
chemicals in the concrete; it wasn't safe to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.040 --> 00:06:01.800
it never had been. She'd worked in nursing homes&nbsp;
and seen the effects of concrete on construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.740
workers. They'd become paralyzed, unable to move,&nbsp;
spending so many years breathing concrete air,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.740 --> 00:06:14.340
letting it absorb into their skin. Their bodies&nbsp;
had slowly started to turn into it. She knew it.

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:18.900
She started to go crazy, pinning up news&nbsp;
clippings, medical journals, photographs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:24.900
maps—anything she could find—all over her&nbsp;
walls, her entire bedroom. She had red string&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.820
hanging from the walls and ceiling, connecting&nbsp;
to every surface. Robert watched helplessly from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:35.340
the doorway, unable to do anything as he saw&nbsp;
his mother steadily entering into psychosis.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:41.340
But the further her paranoia went, the less&nbsp;
energy she seemed to have for it. Weeks went by,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:46.980
and her manic fervor was replaced with lethargy.&nbsp;
She got slower and slower in her movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:52.440
talked less and less at meal times, and began&nbsp;
to sit down for extended chunks of the day.

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:57.660
Robert, assuming that she was tired from&nbsp;
grief, did what he could to help. He carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.660 --> 00:07:01.980
an armchair up to her room and placed it&nbsp;
in the corner by the window so that she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:06.660
could look out over the world around her. He&nbsp;
thought she was resting. But it soon became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:12.540
apparent that she wasn't really resting;&nbsp;
she was withdrawing, almost freezing over.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:17.640
Desperate to try and get his mother to re-engage&nbsp;
with the world, Robert sat down with her to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:22.740
and understand all of her conspiracy theories&nbsp;
around his father's death. She explained slowly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.740 --> 00:07:28.740
and calmly that the concrete being used in New&nbsp;
York was poisoned, cursed. She didn't know where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.020
it had been dug up from, how it had been mixed,&nbsp;
or who was behind it, but she was getting close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:39.840
Maybe it was the US government, maybe it was&nbsp;
the same witches, maybe it was God himself.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.220
But she knew her husband, and she&nbsp;
knew he wouldn't have fallen that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.220 --> 00:07:48.180
day. He had walked across that plank&nbsp;
thousands of times without incident;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:53.340
there was no way he would have made that mistake.&nbsp;
Robert tried to connect the dots of what she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.340 --> 00:07:57.960
saying. Did she really think that it was&nbsp;
the concrete that had killed his father?

00:07:57.960 --> 00:08:02.820
She said that it was and that the&nbsp;
same concrete is now killing her.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:08.160
She showed him her hands, turned them over, front&nbsp;
and back, under the light for him to inspect.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:14.580
They looked just like normal hands—elderly,&nbsp;
wrinkled, with deep veins popping out—but normal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:19.260
She scratched at them and tried to feign&nbsp;
pain for the effects of her fingers.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:25.020
"Look," she said, "you see that powder&nbsp;
coming off them? That's concrete powder."

00:08:25.020 --> 00:08:26.880
But Robert didn't see any of it.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:27.150
*

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:31.380
The concrete man came to the Foundation's&nbsp;
attention a couple of decades ago. Construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:35.820
workers in New York in the late 1990s were&nbsp;
hard at work clearing out the foundations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.680
for a new skyscraper. The site was built on&nbsp;
top of what used to be a residential block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.000
but one intrepid builder soon discovered&nbsp;
something that shook him to his core.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:50.520
There was an old cellar beneath where the building&nbsp;
used to be. The lock on it was heavy, thick with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.980
rust, and hadn't been opened for close to 70&nbsp;
years. He and his team managed to pry it open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.980 --> 00:09:01.560
put on their flashlights, and went inside. What&nbsp;
they found was a basement apartment still sitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:06.360
there beneath the rubble. It was a relic of&nbsp;
its time. Thick dust covered every surface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.900 --> 00:09:11.520
ugly wallpaper surrounded them, and ancient&nbsp;
appliances covered with rust sat in the kitchen.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:14.580
It was almost like walking&nbsp;
through a haunted house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:19.140
The builders joked about ghosts stalking these&nbsp;
corridors as they made their way through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.140 --> 00:09:24.480
until they reached the bedroom. Sliding the&nbsp;
door open, they faced a man sitting there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:30.000
staring at them from an old armchair. The four&nbsp;
burly builders screamed like little girls for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.400
a split second, then burst out laughing when they&nbsp;
realized the man wasn't real. He must have been a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:40.920
waxwork or something. He was sitting so perfectly&nbsp;
still and hadn't reacted at all to their presence.

00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:45.600
They went over to inspect what he was made&nbsp;
out of. They tapped on his forehead, lifted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.820
his arms and dropped them, and were surprised&nbsp;
at how convincing the fake man was. That was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:55.500
of course, until he politely asked them&nbsp;
not to touch him. This time they really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.500 --> 00:10:00.360
did scream like girls, running straight out&nbsp;
of that basement into a local police station.

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:00.780
*

00:10:00.780 --> 00:10:05.460
This basement apartment was where Robert&nbsp;
Chetford moved following the fire in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:10.380
parents' home and the death of his paralyzed&nbsp;
mother. Racked with guilt, he had never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:14.880
quite been able to process what had happened to&nbsp;
cause his mother's mental state to deteriorate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:19.680
so rapidly. By the time she died, she had&nbsp;
been looking at her hands and genuinely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:23.820
believing they were made of concrete.&nbsp;
She would not move, she would not eat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:28.500
she would not sleep. For days, she would&nbsp;
just sit, staring out of her window.

00:10:28.500 --> 00:10:33.600
He knew he should have taken her to a doctor, but&nbsp;
they just couldn't afford it. Maybe if he had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.600 --> 00:10:38.460
this would have all worked out differently.&nbsp;
Robert would think about it hour after hour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:43.620
He would sit down in the armchair in the corner&nbsp;
of his room and ponder what had happened. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:47.340
difficult to keep track of time living&nbsp;
in the basement—there were no windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:50.940
no daylight at all—but he didn't&nbsp;
think he had been sitting there for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:55.140
very long when he noticed the skin&nbsp;
on his hands starting to turn gray.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.780
Without panic, he slowly lifted his hands to&nbsp;
his eyes and inspected what was happening. His&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.780 --> 00:11:06.720
fingers, down to about halfway through his palms,&nbsp;
were dry and solid. He could move them just about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:11.940
but it took a normal effort, and he could see&nbsp;
cracks running along the back of the concrete&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:18.360
when he did. So he decided not to. He wouldn't&nbsp;
say he felt calm exactly; maybe disconnected.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:24.240
He didn't feel particularly hungry, he didn't&nbsp;
feel particularly tired, he didn't feel much at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.240 --> 00:11:30.060
all. His concrete limbs were only a passing&nbsp;
curiosity to him as he sat in his chair.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:34.260
Very occasionally, he would get up&nbsp;
to put a record on his record player,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:37.860
then return to his seat and stare&nbsp;
at the wall. That would be every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.860 --> 00:11:41.700
couple of months at most. The rest&nbsp;
of the time, he would just sit there.

00:11:41.700 --> 00:11:46.080
One day, he noticed in a vague corner&nbsp;
of his mind that the ground was shaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.080 --> 00:11:51.360
and dust was filling the room. The ripping&nbsp;
sound of dynamite and the heavy stone just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:55.260
above his head told him that the apartment&nbsp;
block towering over him had fallen down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.940
and almost come through his ceiling.&nbsp;
Robert just sat there, unconcerned.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:04.440
Another day, some indeterminate time&nbsp;
later, a group of builders came in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:09.960
dressed in peculiar futuristic clothing, with&nbsp;
bright yellow hats and reflective vests. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:14.940
seemed rather startled to see him, though in&nbsp;
truth, he had little care for seeing them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.880
What did perturb him was when a&nbsp;
group of people claiming to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:22.380
agents from the "Foundation" came&nbsp;
into his room and carried him out.

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:27.660
He didn't like the noise, didn't like the&nbsp;
change. If he wanted to move, he would move,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.660 --> 00:12:32.520
not that he could, of course. He was now&nbsp;
entirely made of concrete, but that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.620
beside the point. These people put him in the&nbsp;
back of a van and drove him across half of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.620 --> 00:12:43.800
United States. They wheeled him through brightly&nbsp;
lit rooms and into a disgusting modern white box.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:48.840
They had done the courtesy of bringing his chair&nbsp;
along and found a new record player for him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:54.180
with a whole selection of albums lining a shelf&nbsp;
on the wall. He would have appreciated it and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:59.220
said thank you to them, but he didn't really&nbsp;
care. He wasn't present at all. This was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.220 --> 00:13:02.940
of course, due to the fact that he&nbsp;
was made of concrete by this point.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:07.020
However, once he was sat in his chair&nbsp;
and took in the sight in front of him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:12.420
Robert Chetford felt a dull panic rising in&nbsp;
his chest. They'd placed him at a window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:17.580
just like he'd placed his dying mother in&nbsp;
front of a window. He tried to clench his jaw,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:23.100
tried to move his hands, tried to cry out in&nbsp;
fear and pain, but he couldn't. He was paralyzed.

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:28.200
The smell of smoke filled his chest, choking&nbsp;
him, torturing him as the memories of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.200 --> 00:13:33.480
burning household raged in his mind. He&nbsp;
tried to scream. He tried to fight. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:37.980
tried to do anything other than sit there.&nbsp;
But it was far too late for any of that.

00:13:37.980 --> 00:13:38.280
*

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.760
"Good afternoon, Mr. Chetford," the&nbsp;
researcher said, busying herself and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.480
putting on another album for him to listen&nbsp;
to. "Is there anything I can do for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.480 --> 00:13:47.820
sir? Anything I can do to&nbsp;
make you more comfortable?"

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:52.500
The concrete man said nothing. The researcher&nbsp;
patted him on the back of his hand.

00:13:52.500 --> 00:13:55.860
"Made of concrete indeed. Looks&nbsp;
just like regular skin to me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:59.460
Mr. Chetford. I do wonder what's&nbsp;
going on inside that head of yours."

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:02.340
On her way out of the room, she&nbsp;
glanced at his view from the window.

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:05.520
"Beautiful weather outside&nbsp;
today, isn't it? Scorching even."

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:09.060
Now go check out “SCP-011 - Sentient Civil&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:09.060 --> 00:14:12.960
War Memorial” and “SCP-650 -&nbsp;
Startling Statue” for more!

